 legislation for it is called  the 17th amendment it's in the  Constitution as it is for example the  supposing some kind of crime is  committed and and the police start  investigations then a politician will  intervene and say don't do this because  this is my friend and that's a very  simplistic simplified example but this  kind of thing happening all the time in  others government it has politicized  everything so the 17th amendment is an  attempt to to depoliticize to give  independence to the to the to the media  to the police the judiciary the  administrative system for example the  senior public servants love the sinner  Administrative Service I cannot make  administrative decisions because the  politicians interfere and so this has  been  really destructive of the public  interest so I think parallel to the  search for a peace through devolution  there should also be mechanisms in the  broader social order in particular -  there should be an attempt to put into  effect the 17th amendment in fact the  fact that the amendment has been there  but has not been put into effect is also  illustrative of Sri Lanka's problem that  there is good there are good intentions  but they are not followed through and so  we'll have to think of some way of you  know you know affecting that  follow-through and one possibility is  that religious leaders might be able to  you know exert some pressure and it  should be understood not so much as some  kind of sectarian thing there should be  some unity some kind of I think  communication not only within the  Buddhist different within the different  Buddhist divisions but also among  religions I think if the religious  leadership of all the major religions  and there are four major religions  representing Salonga Buddhism  Christianity Hinduism and Islam if all  these people can get together and exert  an infinite need not be confined to them  there is also interestingly enough civil  society in sri lanka which is very quite  admirable I mean dependently and through  against all gods had been expressing  their waves and coming up with extremely  you know civilized ideas about society  and so if they are can if you can  imagine some sort of civil society  movement of both the clergy of all the  religions and these intellectuals and  other people who were committed to  certain kind of social values I think  something can be done and do you see  local Buddhist leaders who you believe  will take up that call unfortunately the  Buddhist leadership itself is very  partisan and they are themselves not  they have not shown them themselves we  capable of objectively understand the  this paradigm of the myth of political  Buddhism and and its historical nature  and its relation to the economy that  kind of thing they have not been able to  understand and therefore the Buddhist  leadership itself has been rather  disappointing but that is not to say  there are no individual members in that  leadership there are some I think those  people will have to talk to other people  are the leaders of religions plus I  think civil society leaders in the in  the secular society and do you believe  there's a role you noted earlier in our  discussion that the Dalai Lama has taken  a position in his interpretation of  Buddhism that is different than the  prevailing Buddhist theology in Sri  Lanka do you think that other leaders  like the Dalai Lama from other parts of  the Buddhist world could play a role in  Sri Lanka  well conceivably but I think it is  probably better if the initiator comes  from within the country because there it  it is there is a lot of popular you know  xenophobia and there is a distrust  mistrust of foreigners and and so I  think in fact the case of Dalai Lama is  a very interesting case because I have  participated in discussions like this in  different parts of this country in  Washington DC and other places and the  question has come up over and over again  why Cannot Srilanka invited that ILOM  know that's a very big problem because  the dalai lama represent what we call  the mahayana buddhism and sri lanka is  is that there about the Buddhist country  this is these are two major divisions in  Buddhism and the sri lankan buddhist  hierarchy would be very opposed to the  idea of the Lama making any you know  being invited to anything and therefore  he will not although the Lama is  enormous  prestige in the West he does not come on  same kind of prestige in sri lanka i see  then returning to the question of a  resolution drawing on the positive  forces of religion from within Sri Lanka  how would you how would you describe the  obligations that a committed Buddhist in  sri lanka who disagrees with this  prevailing radical and violent  interpretation of buddhist  responsibilities how should such a  person see their responsibilities in sri  lanka  I think they should support the civil  society efforts to to bring about not  only narrowly conceived solution to the  present civil war but the general  problem I think the there's a general  malaise and that is really the problem  might even civil war is a manifestation  or an expression of this general problem  and and the way to address it as I said  is the putting into effect the 7th 17th  amendment to the Constitution and I  think the community leaders that you  have in mind could if they can somehow  communicate with each other then inform  themselves into some effective force and  then join hands with these religious  leaders and others civil society leaders  that is the way an individual I think  could help I see and and I ask on that  note it seems that throughout our  discussion we're returning to the theme  that what is really needed to establish  peace in Sri Lanka his work on the the  government and the public policy side  from a number of aspects with religion  as part of that part of the background  perhaps but what we seem to be saying is  that it's not a religious dialogue as  such that will actually help to overcome  this conflict is that fair to summarize  yes I think I think that would be  because it is not to begin with anything  to do with religious ideas per se it is  nothing to do with spiritual and moral  system at all it is it is much more to  do with the politicization political  Buddhism and for example in the lima  once I count the king before going to  war says this I'm not going to this is  not a fight for my glory this is this is  for I'm fighting for Buddhism and this  should sound rather familiar to you in  this election season with but the king  is saying religion first me second you  know that's right that's right  absolutely and what what lessons do you  think if any this approach that we're  talking about to resolve the situation  Sri Lanka what what lessons does that  provide for other conflicts in which  religion plays a role or would you  rather argue that each one should be  looked at separately oh definitely each  one should be looked at separately but I  would suspect from general sociological  point of view that that it needs to be  addressed in a kind of a total istic way  I would also suspect just like in Sri  Lanka there are forces outside the  sphere of religion itself and that are  very crucial in the in the in the in the  creation in the etiology of these  conflicts and they should be addressed  I say well thank you very much for your  thoughts and thank you for coming to  speak with the students in faith and  globalization today thank you very much  nice to be here
 

